      "ts": 1793721895000,
      "fullText": "In Brief Posted: 8:04 AM PST · November 3, 2026 Image Credits: Ethan Miller / Getty Images Zack Whittaker New York City&#8217;s Mamdani criticizes Madison Square Garden owner over surveillance tech Mayor of New York City Zohran Mamdani has criticized the owner of Madison Square Garden, one of the city&#8217;s largest entertainment venues, arguing that the company&#8217;s surveillance technology should not be allowed. Mamdani told Wired in an interview on Tuesday that surveillance technologies are &#8220;indicative of much that is wrong in our country today.&#8221; He added that City Council member Shahana Hanif introduced two pieces of local legislation aimed at banning the collection of biometric data , such as using facial recognition, in public places across the city. Mamdani said that the draft legislation is &#8220;an important response to this kind of an issue.&#8221; The comments come after a stream of reporting from Wired detailing how Madison Square Garden&#8217;s owner James Dolan relies on surveillance cameras and face-scanning technology to track people across and outside of its venues. In one report , Wired revealed that Dolan&#8217;s company keeps a database that, among other things, marks some visitors, including celebrity performers, as &#8220;LGBTQIA,&#8221; for reasons that are unclear. Dolan subsequently sued Wired over its reporting. In 2021, MSG made headlines after a woman was banned from Radio City, another Dolan-owned venue, after being recognized by facial recognition systems as an attorney at a law firm who was suing Dolan&#8217;s company but was not involved in the litigation. When reached by TechCrunch, Madison Square Garden spokesperson Mikyl Cordova said: &#8220;Wired’s reporting is inaccurate and false. MSG is pursuing legal remedies,&#8221; without providing evidence for its claim. Since 2021, New York City has required businesses to disclose when they are collecting biometric data from visitors. Updated with comment from MSG. Topics "
